---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site, pillars } from "../data/site.js";
const eq = pillars.find((p) => p.slug === "equity-compensation");

const title = "Employer Equity Planning | ISOs, RSUs, QSBS | Advisant";
const description =
  "Tax planning for stock options, RSUs, ESPPs, and founder stock: AMT modeling on ISO exercises, 83(b) elections, QSBS tracking, and equity in an acquisition.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Service",
  "@id": `${site.domain}/equity-compensation/#service`,
  name: "Employer Equity Planning",
  description,
  provider: { "@id": `${site.domain}/#organization` },
  areaServed: [
    { "@type": "State", name: "Colorado" },
    { "@type": "State", name: "Michigan" },
  ],
};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/equity-compensation/" schema={schema}>

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="hero__grid">
        <div>
          <p class="formline formline--onDark">Employer equity</p>
          <h1>Equity compensation punishes people who wait.</h1>
          <p>
            The alternative minimum tax on an ISO exercise is calculated on a spread you never
            received in cash. An 83(b) election is worthless on day 31. Qualified small business
            stock requires facts that must be true at issuance. None of these are fixable at
            filing.
          </p>
          <div class="hero__actions">
            <a class="btn" href="/contact/">New client application</a>
            <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/insights/iso-tax-when-your-company-is-acquired/">ISOs in an acquisition</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <aside class="credential">
          <div class="credential__seal" aria-hidden="true">EA</div>
          <h2>Model it before you exercise.</h2>
          <p>
            We run the numbers against your full picture — other income, state residency,
            expected liquidity — and tell you what a decision costs before you make it.
          </p>
          <dl>
            <dt>Covers</dt><dd>ISO, NSO, RSU, ESPP</dd>
            <dt>Also</dt><dd>Founder stock, QSBS</dd>
          </dl>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="formline">Where it goes wrong</p>
        <h2>Four decisions with hard deadlines</h2>

        <h3>Exercising incentive stock options</h3>
        <p>
          Exercise and hold, and the spread becomes an AMT preference item — tax owed on paper
          gain, in cash, in a year you received nothing. Exercise and sell, and you have a
          disqualifying disposition taxed entirely as ordinary income. The right answer depends
          on your other income, whether the company is liquid, and how much risk you can carry
          on a single position. It is a model, not a rule of thumb.
        </p>

        <h3>The 83(b) election</h3>
        <p>
          Thirty days from the transfer of restricted stock. Not thirty business days, not
          thirty days from when someone told you about it. Filed late, it does not exist, and
          the difference on a successful company is measured in years of ordinary income
          instead of capital gain. We file it and keep the mailing evidence, because the IRS
          does not acknowledge receipt.
        </p>

        <h3>Qualified small business stock</h3>
        <p>
          Section 1202 can exclude substantial gain, but the requirements attach at issuance —
          the corporation's gross assets, the trade or business, the original issue — and the
          five-year holding period runs from there. Founders routinely discover at sale that
          nobody documented the facts that would have supported the position. The time to
          establish it is the day the stock is issued.
        </p>

        <h3>RSU withholding</h3>
        <p>
          Employers commonly withhold at the statutory supplemental rate, which is well below
          the marginal rate of a high earner with a large vest. The shortfall shows up in April
          as a bill nobody warned you about, sometimes with an underpayment penalty attached.
          It is entirely predictable and entirely preventable with a quarterly estimate.
        </p>

        <h2>Equity in a transaction</h2>
        <p>
          Acquisitions turn options into ordinary income more often than people expect, and an
          acceleration clause can silently convert part of an ISO grant into NSOs by pushing
          past the $100,000 annual limit. We work alongside your attorney rather than reacting
          to closing documents.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/insights/iso-tax-when-your-company-is-acquired/">
            Read the full breakdown of what happens to ISOs in an acquisition &rarr;
          </a>
        </p>

        <h2>What this covers</h2>
        <ul>{eq.points.map((x) => <li>{x}</li>)}</ul>
      </div>

      <aside class="facts">
        <p class="formline">Common triggers</p>
        <ul class="ticklist">
          <li>A first grant, or options you have not exercised</li>
          <li>A tender offer or secondary sale</li>
          <li>Leaving a company with vested options</li>
          <li>An AMT bill you did not see coming</li>
          <li>Founder stock issued in the last five years</li>
          <li>A large RSU vest scheduled this year</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="btn" href="/contact/">New client application</a>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4rem;font-size:.9rem">
          <a href="/fit-check/">Advisor or preparer? Take the fit check &rarr;</a>
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
